<BaseLayout title="About MetadataView — A Local Metadata Privacy Toolkit" description="Why MetadataView exists, what its browser-only tools do, and where this open MVP draws the line." path="/about/">
  <article class="content-page"><header><span class="eyebrow">About the toolkit</span><h1>Files talk. This site translates.</h1><p>A photo can carry GPS. A PDF can name its author. MetadataView makes those details visible before you share the file.</p></header><div class="manifesto"><div><span>01</span><h2>Local first</h2><p>If a modern browser can do the job safely, the file should not take a detour through somebody else’s server.</p></div><div><span>02</span><h2>Explain the verdict</h2><p>A privacy score without reasons is just a mood. Every risk points to fields and a next action.</p></div><div><span>03</span><h2>Admit the edge</h2><p>Metadata is editable. Credentials have scope. Pixels can leak details. The copy says that out loud.</p></div></div><section class="about-tools"><span class="section-index">THE MVP</span><h2>Four complete paths, not four mockups</h2><p>The universal viewer handles six formats. Image privacy rules score hidden exposure. The remover creates and verifies a cleaned copy. The C2PA page dynamically loads the official browser verifier.</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button button-primary" href="/metadata-viewer/">Inspect a file</a><a class="button button-secondary" href="/privacy/">Read the privacy design</a></div></section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
